WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.717
The best mental model is to treat the inner query as a result producer.

00:00:04.717 --> 00:00:07.292
The inner query produces something.

00:00:07.292 --> 00:00:10.447
The outer query then uses that result.

00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:17.362
The result might be one value, a set of values, or rows that behave like an intermediate table.

00:00:17.362 --> 00:00:22.332
Before writing the SQL, ask what shape the outer query needs.

00:00:22.332 --> 00:00:26.407
That question prevents many common subquery errors.

00:00:26.407 --> 00:00:32.527
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:32.527 --> 00:00:38.622
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:38.622 --> 00:00:46.782
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:46.782 --> 00:00:53.032
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:53.032 --> 00:00:59.200
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
